# Self-hosting env for docker-compose.yml — copy to `.env` and fill in.
# Do NOT commit the filled-in .env (it holds secrets).

# --- public identity ---
# Your domain (must have an A/AAAA record pointing at this host BEFORE `up`, so
# Let's Encrypt can issue a cert).
DOMAIN=call.example.org
# Email for Let's Encrypt expiry notices.
ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.org

# --- TURN (coturn) ---
# Long random shared secret for TURN-REST credentials. Generate one:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Required: docker compose refuses to start while it is empty.
TURN_SECRET=
# The server's PUBLIC IP (coturn advertises this as the relay address).
#   curl -s https://api.ipify.org
TURN_EXTERNAL_IP=203.0.113.10
# By default the browser also contacts Google and Cloudflare STUN servers (a third party sees
# the caller's IP address). Uncomment to use none (or a comma-separated list of your own).
# The public relay openrelay.metered.ca is only used when no own TURN is configured; this
# stack configures one, and TURN_PUBLIC_FALLBACK=off makes sure it is never handed out.
# STUN_URLS=none
# TURN_PUBLIC_FALLBACK=off

# --- edition ---
# community (default) or pro. Community is the full product, group calls included. `pro` adds three capabilities
# that need a commercial licence (LICENSE-COMMERCIAL.md); the list below says which.
EUDI_EDITION=community

# --- switching capabilities off ---
# Comma-separated capability ids to disable. Empty (or unset) means: everything this edition
# includes is on.
#
# This exists for compliance, not for pricing: "file transfer during a citizen consultation is
# not permitted" is a sentence out of a real data protection concept, and an operator must be
# able to honour it. It therefore applies to Pro capabilities too — holding a licence does not
# mean you want every function on.
#
# Six capabilities refuse to be switched off (marked below). That is deliberate: a guarantee the
# operator can turn off is not a guarantee. If the attribute whitelist were disableable, nobody
# could infer from the presence of this software that it is being honoured — which is its whole
# purpose.
#
# A typo does not fail silently: an unknown id is reported at startup rather than ignored.
# The authoritative live answer for a running instance is GET /capabilities.
#
# EUDI_FEATURES_OFF=file-transfer,emoji

# >>> capabilities — generated from src/core/features.ts, do not edit by hand
#
# ALWAYS ON — cannot be switched off: the product itself, and the guarantees:
#   calls                Video/Audio/Chat calls
#   eudi-verification    EUDI PID verification + privacy gate
#   self-host            Self-hosting stack
#   wrp-policy           WRP attribute whitelist, purpose binding + public /wrp-policy descriptor
#   pseudonym-id         Ephemeral pairwise pseudonym (pseudonym_id, server-derived)
#   audit-metadata       PII-free verification audit log (metadata only, every edition)
#
# INCLUDED IN THIS EDITION — switch off any of these with EUDI_FEATURES_OFF:
#   copy-link            Copy invite link
#   planned-invites      Scheduled calls (signed invitation, role-bound)
#   qes-in-call          In-call document sharing & signing ceremony (QES)
#   channel-binding      Channel binding: identity tied to the connection DTLS key (SD-JWT VC, currently disabled)
#   screen-share         Screen sharing
#   calendar-invite      Calendar invite (.ics/Google)
#   native-share         Native share (Web Share)
#   meeting-controls     Mute / camera toggles
#   spotlight            Spotlight / stage layout (auto-focus on screen-share, fluid stage, per-tile fullscreen)
#   hand-raise           Raise hand (signal over the chat DataChannel)
#   file-transfer        File transfer over DataChannel
#   emoji                Emoji picker
#   wallet-pass          Appointment pass (Apple Wallet) for scheduled calls
#   embed                Embeddable iframe widget
#   multi-party          Multi-party calls (full mesh)
#   multi-credential     Multi-wallet / multi-format verification (mdoc + SD-JWT VC)
#   in-call-credentials  In-call credential requests (catalogue)
#
# Modules (EUDI_MODULES) bring further capabilities and list them in their own manifest.
# GET /capabilities shows everything that is active, modules included.
#
# <<< end of generated block

# Two capabilities are additionally CONFIG-gated — they stay off until you configure them, and
# the browser then never offers a button that dead-ends:
#   planned-invites   needs EUDI_INVITE_SECRET (signs appointment links; without it a plain
#                     room link is used, which does not survive a restart as an appointment)
#   wallet-pass       needs EUDI_PASS_* (an Apple Wallet signing certificate of your own)
# See .env.example for both, and enabledFeatures() in src/server.ts for the exact rule.

# --- verification: three ways to run ---
#   1. Just try the call, no registration:     EUDI_VERIFIER=none
#      Every call is anonymous; the interface says so with a permanent banner. Leave the
#      EUDIPLO_* lines below empty or remove them.
#   2. Click through the verification flow:    docker compose --profile mock up -d
#      with EUDIPLO_BASE_URL=http://mock-eudiplo:8080, EUDI_ALLOW_MOCK_VERIFIER=true (without it
#      the app refuses to start against the dummy) and non-placeholder values for
#      EUDIPLO_CLIENT_ID / EUDIPLO_CLIENT_SECRET (the dummy ignores their content, but the app
#      sends no request without them; leave the EUDIPLO_ROOT_* lines unset).
#   3. Real operation:                         your own EUDIPLO instance, see below.
# EUDI_VERIFIER=none

# --- EUDIPLO verifier ---
# YOUR OWN EUDIPLO instance — this application ships no verifier and cannot verify without one.
# What you need (see README for the walkthrough):
#   - EUDIPLO 9.1.0 (ghcr.io/eudiplo/eudiplo), PINNED, not :latest — one major at a time,
#     admin UI in the same version, back up the data volume before upgrading
#   - its own subdomain with TLS: the wallet contacts the verifier directly
#   - an ACCESS CERTIFICATE from a registration authority, issued for that hostname
#     (in Germany: the German Registrar). Allow weeks for this — it is an application,
#     not a download, and without it real wallets refuse the request.
#   - VP_REMOVE_TA=true on the instance, or wallets that reject `trusted_authorities` fail
# For frontend work only there is a dummy without cryptography (`--profile mock`,
# http://mock-eudiplo:8080). It is NOT a verifier: it also returns data that was never
# requested, so protocol errors stay invisible. Never point a real deployment at it: it accepts
# any name, and the app only starts against it with EUDI_ALLOW_MOCK_VERIFIER=true (trial only).
EUDIPLO_BASE_URL=https://verifier.example.org
# Legacy root-credential path, deprecated (see src/eudiplo.ts): leave these unset and use the
# tenant client below.
# EUDIPLO_ROOT_ID=your-root-id
# EUDIPLO_ROOT_SECRET=__set_me__
EUDIPLO_CLIENT_ID=your-tenant-id
# The tenant client's secret (recommended); the ROOT pair above is the deprecated legacy path.
EUDIPLO_CLIENT_SECRET=__set_me__
